#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048978 :topic/id #uuid "d0b65e3a-175c-5881-9e6b-528af522aa34" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184070 :question/id #uuid "10cc01a6-af19-5340-9743-ade4d5e252dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5425" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184071 :question/id #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5426" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\left. \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\;\\right| \\;x_1, x_3 \\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}?\n$$\n\n \n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\;\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} }\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184124 :question/id #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9963" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3836 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049895}] :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem"}]}
